However, automated tools are capable of detecting only a portion of the accessibility issues present on a given page. Analysis of axe-core — the engine underlying tools such as Google Lighthouse — found that automated rules could be applied to approximately 16 out of 50 WCAG 2.1 Level AA success criteria, supporting industry estimates of 20–30% coverage when measured by criteria count. When measured by volume of real-world issues rather than criteria count, the same analysis found automated testing identified approximately 57% of issues found during full audits, as certain failure types occur with disproportionate frequency. Academic research evaluating multiple tools against identical content found that even running six automated tools in parallel left half of all WCAG 2 success criteria uncovered, with an estimated six in ten violations missed. A further independent analysis concluded that approximately 42% of WCAG success criteria cannot be detected through automated scanning at all, as they require human judgement about the quality, appropriateness, or usability of content.However, automated tools are capable of detecting only a portion of the accessibility issues present on a given page. Analysis of axe-core — the engine underlying tools such as Google Lighthouse — found that automated rules could be applied to approximately 16 out of 50 WCAG 2.1 Level AA success criteria, supporting industry estimates of 20–30% coverage when measured by criteria count.
